+  /* LOCKSTEP: the simulation step must not come from this machine's clock.
+   *
+   * nextFrameTime above is "how many video fields the clock says elapsed", and it is what
+   * drives the world forward. That is per-process by construction: on the synthetic clock it
+   * comes out of a counter the audio thread also increments, and under GETV_REALCLOCK=1 it is
+   * host time and moves with load. Two machines running the same inputs therefore advance the
+   * world by different amounts and diverge with nobody touching a controller, which is exactly
+   * what a two-process trial shows -- the RNG state differs at the same tick whenever the two
+   * ran at different frame rates, and matches every time when they did not.
+   *
+   * gePlayerPinDelta() was written for this and nothing had ever called it. With a session
+   * open, every machine advances by the same pinned number of fields per pass, so the step is
+   * a decision rather than a measurement. The renderer is untouched and still runs free; only
+   * what the simulation is told has elapsed is fixed.
+   *
+   * Perfect Dark's netplay branch avoids this question rather than answering it: its clients do
+   * not simulate at all, they are sent entity state (port/src/net/netmsg.c, SVC_PROP_MOVE and
+   * friends). Lockstep does not have that option -- every machine simulates, so every machine
+   * must take the same step. */
+  {
+      extern int gePlayerPinnedDelta(void);
+      const s32 pinned = (s32) gePlayerPinnedDelta();
+      if (pinned > 0) { nextFrameTime = (u32) pinned; }
+  }
+
